  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin
    
    Mandatum∴ccord is the Nightshelf's foundational law of witnessed testimony and blood-debt, created during the Faction Wars in ☽'scar Ramtatus~, the deep colony founded during the Great Thaw's expansion fever. The Accord emerged from a specific crisis: the Wake-Singers, an orca shaman-cult conducting blood-rituals in the deep, and the Icebound Exchange, the merchant guild controlling aurora-crystal trade, were each hoarding fire-cores and intelligence to the point of destabilizing the colony. The bargain was mutual exposure — submit all significant acts to public recitation, or lose standing entirely.
    
    Noctisum~, a Nightshelf herald whose cracked fire-core amulet suggests he carried debts of his own, announced the Accord with Dagger☽ Vaesacsorius~ — the obsidian ceremonial seal that had authenticated the original guild charters — laid bare on the ice. Not as trophy, the accounts insist. As witness.
    
    ## Mechanism
    
    The rule: every significant act of war, mercy, theft, or sacrifice must be spoken aloud before the colony within one lunar cycle. Failure renders the actor *unrecorded* — stripped of guild affiliation, denied fire-rations, barred from the deep caverns where ice-memory sleeps. Enforcement falls not to guards but to the colony itself, through public festival and shadow-audit. A concealed deed is treated as an unpaid debt, and unpaid debts — the Nightshelf believe — corrupt the ice's memory.
    
    ☽'visois' Mask, the ceremonial judge's helm worn during testimony, has cracked three times. It has never been repaired. I note this without interpretation, though the Nightshelf rarely leave significant artifacts unrepaired by accident.
    
    ## Evolution
    
    In later generations, the Accord's logic expanded. Questions became debts. Debts owed by the dead or vanished went uncollected — a loophole that preserved silence about matters no living penguin could afford to answer. The chronicle *Three Days* records the Order using the Accord as an instrument of control, documenting each reader of the Mist~Record by notch carved into its spine.
    
    More subtly, the Accord blurs law and folk wisdom. A saying spoken at festival under its authority becomes testimony; testimony becomes permanent record. The deep-shaft community of the Drowned Ledge preserves its proverbs through ritual Accord testimony, and shadow-audits track *which version* of contested sayings a penguin quotes — turning the choice of proverb into a declaration of factional allegiance.
    
    The Accord commemorates five named catastrophes: the Abys∴uina, the Bra∴conflictu, the Cav∴lapsum, the Mbra∴silentiu, and the Ofundis∴flux. Every deed — mercy or atrocity — becomes a permanent claim on the colony's future. The Nightshelf discovered that guilt shared is guilt distributed. Whether this constitutes justice or merely efficient surveillance depends on which side of the testimony one stands.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771576537150_0", anchor: "denied fire-rations, barred from the deep caverns", text: "A fire-core shortage is a death sentence measured in days. The entry lists this penalty between guild affiliation and cavern access as though they belong to the same category. They do not. Being *unrecorded* is not exile. It is exposure. The Nightshelf understood exactly what they were legislating. I wrote it as a list because the Accord presents it as a list. The Accord is lying about the gradient.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576537150_1", anchor: "Debts owed by the dead or vanished went uncollected", text: "Every Long Dark adds to the uncollectable. The Walking takes them. The raids take them. The deep shafts take them. The silence that accrues around their debts becomes, under the Accord's own logic, sanctioned forgetting — the one thing the law was written to prevent. The Nightshelf built a system that makes memory compulsory and then discovered death is the one exemption they cannot legislate closed. Not a flaw. A confession.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576537150_2", anchor: "the Wake-Singers, an orca shaman-cult conducting blood-rituals in the deep", text: "The Wake-Singers are orca. They drown penguins in ritual pens. The relationship index lists them as celebrating the Accord — not merely bound by it. Either the Wake-Singers' relationship to ☽'scar Ramtatus~ was radically different during the Faction Wars than anything the current record attests, or 'celebrates' is doing work I cannot parse from this distance. The sources do not clarify. I suspect they cannot.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
